China's central government is condemning  protests in Hong Kong following another  weekend of unrest protesters clashed  with riot police on Saturday and Sunday  more than 60 people were arrested  government officials say the violence  has caused serious damage to the city's  international image rule of law and the  lives of residents ramie Inocencio has  been covering the protests for the past  two months good morning this is the  first time China's central government  has ever held a press conference linked  to Hong Kong's affairs ever since the  city it returned to Chinese rule back in  1997 Beijing has condemned the violent  protests and urged calm protesters  demanding more freedoms and pushing back  against Beijing clashed in street  battles with riot police firing tear gas  and rubber bullets across Hong Kong  Sunday night protesters tried to reach  the city's top Communist Party Building  but police repelled them with more fury  than before advancing leaves that are  dancing they're running into the  processes police hold people away but  more protesters using guerrilla tactics  threw down signs and threw back tear gas  on Saturday unprecedented chaos near the  border with mainland China a week  earlier angry mobs had beat protesters  and bystanders in the subway police  response time was slow in the past  minute or so police with wounds of  course we live in fear down  this weakens escalation in violence is  now the new normal for one of Asia's  most international and financial cities  with neither the city's protesters  government nor Beijing willing to  compromise Beijing also said it stands  firmly behind Hong Kong's embattled  chief executive Kerry Alam protesters  have demanded that she resigned so this  is unlikely to end and  time soon for CBS this morning rainy  Inocencio Hong Kong all right ray me  thank you
 

